00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:07.770
TE-SSL: time and event-aware self-supervised learning, for
analysing how Alzheimer's disease progresses.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:15.948
From West Virginia University with Pittsburgh and Aberdeen,
using data from the Alzheimer's Disease Neuroimaging

00:00:16.398 --> 00:00:27.678
The idea is small and specific: contrastive pretraining
already uses class labels when it has them. This paper asks

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:38.676
Roughly 6.7 million Americans over sixty-five live with
Alzheimer's dementia, and MRI-based deep learning has made

00:00:39.126 --> 00:00:50.814
But detection is arguably the easier question. What changes
management is knowing which patients who are currently

00:00:51.264 --> 00:00:55.752
That is a progression question, and it needs a different kind
of label.

00:00:56.202 --> 00:01:02.370
Not a class, but a pair: how long until the event, and whether
the event was actually observed.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:08.160
This is the setup, and the censoring part is what makes it
awkward.

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:18.474
Patient A converts to Alzheimer's dementia at eighteen months.
The event was observed, so the label is the pair: eighteen,

00:01:18.924 --> 00:01:25.164
Patient B converts at forty-two. Same kind of label, longer
time.

00:01:25.614 --> 00:01:37.902
Patient C leaves the study at thirty-six months without
converting. We do not know what happened afterwards. That is

00:01:38.352 --> 00:01:45.360
Patient D finishes the full sixty months event-free. Also
censored, just at the horizon.

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:59.298
The goal is to model the survival function: the probability
that the event has not occurred by time t. And censored

00:02:00.648 --> 00:02:04.128
Here is the whole framework as the authors draw it.

00:02:04.578 --> 00:02:10.098
Images are augmented into multiple views and encoded into
representations z.

00:02:10.548 --> 00:02:15.564
The contrastive objective then pulls some pairs together and
pushes others apart.

00:02:16.014 --> 00:02:24.558
What TE-SSL adds is the delta terms: the time difference
between each pair of samples, which modulates how strongly

00:02:25.008 --> 00:02:30.936
Everything else — the encoder, the augmentations, the
projection head — is standard.

00:02:32.286 --> 00:02:37.782
It is worth being precise about what changes, because the
paper builds it in three steps.

00:02:38.232 --> 00:02:51.840
Plain self-supervised learning has no labels at all. Only an
image and its own augmentation are a positive pair. Every

00:02:52.290 --> 00:03:11.058
Event-aware SSL, which they call E-SSL, uses the event
indicator. Now every patient who converts is a positive for

00:03:11.508 --> 00:03:26.676
But it throws away something obvious. A patient converting at
twenty-one months and one converting at twenty-six are at a

00:03:27.126 --> 00:03:39.174
TE-SSL weights the pull by the time gap. Close in time, strong
pull. Same event but far apart, weak pull. Censored, pushed

00:03:40.524 --> 00:03:44.604
The weight itself is a straight line, fitted to each batch.

00:03:45.054 --> 00:03:49.446
Take the time gap between the two samples, delta i j.

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:57.624
Rescale it using the smallest and largest gaps present in that
batch, so the mapping adapts to whatever spread the batch

00:03:58.074 --> 00:04:06.426
Alpha and beta set the ends: the closest pair in the batch
gets the largest weight alpha, the most distant pair gets the

00:04:06.876 --> 00:04:13.404
It is a simple construction, and being batch-relative is the
part that makes it work without tuning per dataset.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:18.426
Dropped into the loss, the change is one factor.

00:04:18.876 --> 00:04:27.732
This is the supervised contrastive objective — sum over
anchors, average over that anchor's positives, log of a

00:04:28.182 --> 00:04:34.470
The only difference is omega multiplying each exponential, in
both the numerator and the denominator.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:44.808
Set every omega to one and you have supervised contrastive
learning exactly. Remove the label-based positive set as well

00:04:45.258 --> 00:04:50.682
So the three methods in this paper are nested, which makes the
comparison unusually clean.

00:04:52.032 --> 00:05:02.592
The data is ADNI: 493 unique patients, each with one or more
visits, giving 2,007 three-dimensional T1-weighted scans.

00:05:03.042 --> 00:05:14.082
Every visit carries a diagnosis — cognitively normal, mild
cognitive impairment, or Alzheimer's dementia — and the number

00:05:14.532 --> 00:05:21.156
A converter is someone who was normal or mildly impaired at
their first visit and developed AD during the study.

00:05:21.606 --> 00:05:28.086
Splits are made by participant rather than by scan, so no
patient's images appear on both sides.

00:05:28.536 --> 00:05:43.920
Worth registering the scale, though: 493 patients is small for
contrastive pretraining, which typically wants very large

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:53.430
The headline result is a clean ladder on the time-dependent
concordance index, which measures whether the model ranks

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:58.488
No pretraining at all: 0.7329.

00:05:58.938 --> 00:06:07.194
Plain self-supervised pretraining lifts that to 0.7511 — so
SSL helps here even with no labels.

00:06:07.644 --> 00:06:12.012
Adding the event label gets 0.7720.

00:06:12.462 --> 00:06:23.694
And adding time as well gets 0.7873. Each supervisory signal
contributes, and the two together contribute most, which is

00:06:25.044 --> 00:06:29.196
The second metric is worth looking at properly rather than
skimming.

